[
    {
        "content": "<p>In a previous thread, someone showed how users can customize their preferred notation for lambdas</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Term</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"β\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bs</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">funBinder</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\", \"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">bs</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fun x =&gt; x</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I would like to know how applicable this is for other keywords in Lean like <code>structure</code> and <code>def</code> (though I know it may not be recommended, I still want to know what is possible).</p>\n<p>As an example, would it be possible to rename <code>structure</code> to <code>struct</code>, and retain all other parts of the syntax like <code>where</code> and <code>extends</code>, and use-case scenarios that the built-in <code>structure</code> has? Purely a cosmetic change:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">struct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Point</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>What are the limits? Which keywords can and can't be replaced cosmetically?</p>",
        "id": 603420581,
        "sender_full_name": "Sven River",
        "timestamp": 1781539449
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, you can add this kind of things. What is hard is to <em>remove</em> things.</p>",
        "id": 603426993,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1781540728
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For instance, <a href=\"https://github.com/PatrickMassot/verbose-lean4/blob/master/Verbose/English/Examples.lean\">https://github.com/PatrickMassot/verbose-lean4/blob/master/Verbose/English/Examples.lean</a> is an actual valid Lean 4 file which uses custom syntax for top-level declaration (replacing the <code>example</code> and <code>theorem</code> syntax, and the <code>by</code> syntax, in addition to having custom tactics).</p>",
        "id": 603427468,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1781540818
    },
